halro for Debian
================

Installing this package does not initialize configuration and does not start a
service. Nothing below happens on its own.

What the package placed
-----------------------

  /usr/bin/halro                        the gateway binary
  /usr/share/halro/config.example.yaml  an example configuration, not active
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/halro.service a unit, installed but not enabled
  /var/lib/halro                        the data directory, mode 0700,
                                        owned by the system user `halro`

The system user and group `halro` were created without a password and without a
login shell. The unit runs as that user and reads /etc/halro/config.yaml, which
this package deliberately does not create: a configuration that appeared by
itself would be one nobody reviewed.

First run
---------

  sudo install -d -m 0700 -o halro -g halro /etc/halro
  sudo install -m 0600 -o halro -g halro \
    /usr/share/halro/config.example.yaml /etc/halro/config.yaml

Now edit /etc/halro/config.yaml. Two of its values have to change before the
unit can work, because the example is written for running Halro from a
directory of your own rather than under systemd:

  storage.data_dir           ./data        ->  /var/lib/halro/data
  storage.master_key.file    ./master.key  ->  /var/lib/halro/master.key

The unit runs with ProtectSystem=strict and ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/halro, so a
relative path resolves somewhere the service cannot write and the start fails.

The data directory is a *subdirectory* of /var/lib/halro on purpose. Halro takes
an exclusive lock beside its data directory, in the parent — one process, one
data directory, enforced by the filesystem. Setting data_dir to /var/lib/halro
itself puts that lock in /var/lib, which the halro user cannot write, and `halro
init` stops with:

  open publication lock: open /var/lib/.halro-publication-....lock: permission denied

The example's three listeners are on loopback; that is a decision to review, not
a default to accept without reading.

  sudo -u halro halro config check --config /etc/halro/config.yaml
  sudo -u halro halro init --config /etc/halro/config.yaml
  sudo systemctl enable --now halro

`halro init` creates the master key. It is not recoverable if it is lost, and a
backup of it belongs somewhere other than the data directory it protects. See
the backup and restore guide before putting real traffic through the instance.

The Admin Console
-----------------

`halro start` prints a one-time Admin setup token on stdout. Under systemd that
stdout is the journal, where the token persists and is readable by anyone who
can read the journal. Prefer configuring `admin.setup_token_file` before
enabling the unit, or complete the offline `halro admin bootstrap` flow, rather
than harvesting the token from `journalctl -u halro`.

An instance that is reachable beyond loopback, has no administrator yet, and has
no setup-token file refuses to serve. That refusal is the fail-closed outcome,
not a fault.

The dead-man watchdog
---------------------

`halro-deadman` is a separate package, deployed outside Halro's own failure
domain — ideally on a different host. Installing it beside the gateway defeats
its purpose.

